Id: no-other-plugin-storage
Pattern: (\.PluginsPath\b|\.PluginConfigurationsPath\b|Assembly\.Load[A-Za-z]*\(|AppDomain\.CurrentDomain|\bType\.GetType\()
Subject: *.cs
Except: :!*ApplicationPathsThatRefuseEveryCallButThePluginDirectories.cs :!*CallsTheServerSeesTests.cs
Issue: 69

Neither this plugin nor a requests plugin reads the other's storage. That is
what keeps either of them installable alone, and it is the position
`docs/decisions/0002-this-plugin-owns-the-catalogue.md` argues at length. Until
this rule landed that note was the whole of the enforcement, and it said so.

The two spellings are the two ways one plugin reaches another's data, and they
are different failures with the same result.

The first is a path. Every plugin's data folder is a directory under the plugins
path, and every plugin's configuration document is a file under the plugin
configurations path, so composing a path from either one is how a plugin reads a
neighbour's files. This plugin needs neither: the server hands it its own folder,
derived from the assembly it loaded, and the base class writes its own
configuration without being asked where. That is what
`docs/decisions/0003-the-catalogue-lives-in-the-plugins-own-data-folder.md`
decided, and it is what makes this half expressible as a pattern rather than as
a guess. Before that decision there was no store, so there was no legitimate use
of these members to distinguish an illegitimate one from.

The second is an assembly. A plugin can reach another plugin's types without a
reference by loading the assembly at run time and asking it for a type by name,
which is the spelling `no-sibling-plugin-reference` cannot see: that rule reads
project files and source for one name, and a load by path carries no name it
matches.

Two files in the test project are excepted and both are the seam to the server
rather than to a plugin. One is the fake standing in for the server's path
interface, which has to declare every member of it, including the two this rule
names. The other is the test asserting which of those members the plugin
actually reaches while it is constructed, which is the assertion that says the
plugin reads one directory and no more. Excepting the fake and keeping the
assertion would leave the assertion refused for naming the members it exists to
count. Both are named by file suffix rather than by path, so the rename in #14
does not silence them: a file that moves keeps its exception and a file that is
renamed loses it and reddens.

What it cannot do. It reads tracked text one line at a time, so a path built
across two lines, a member reached through a local variable assigned earlier, or
a directory walked upward from this plugin's own folder are all invisible to it.
Nothing it matches is proof of intent either: a legitimate use of these members
would fail this rule and the answer is to argue the exception here rather than
to widen the pattern. And the property it holds is about this tree rather than
about what runs: a plugin that reads another's files through an API the server
offers, rather than through a path, is outside every pattern this lint can write.
